`5			Microware Screen Control Package				The Microware screen package is a UNIX curses compatible (BSD and V)terminal control package offering enhanced functionality.  The screenpackage uses termcap and allows a great degree of control over thescreen display.	ARCHITECTURE	The screen package allows the display of text on the screen in onesingle large window or multiple windows.  Text in a window is stored in aBuffer. Windows and Buffers are separable elements, and multiple windowsmay display text from a single buffer.  Windows and Buffers are notnecessarily restricted in size and a Window does not need to be thesame size as a buffer it is associated with.	A Window is a display area with the following attributes :		Size		Position on the Terminal screen		Buffer from which to obtain text		Position in the Buffer			A Buffer is a block of textual information with the following attributes :		Size		Number of Windows linked to this Buffer		This format suggests that the relationship between Windows, Buffers,and the terminal screen can be stated as follows :		A Buffer is an arbitrarily sized block of text, and a Window 	manipulates some portion of that text and/or displays some portion	of that text on the terminal screen.	It is quite possible to have a Window that is never displayed, but isused only to manipulate the text in the associated Buffer.  Another Windowis then used to display the text.	UNIX CURSES COMPATIBILITY		Curses compatibility is maintained throughout the screen package.  Allcurses-compatible functions operate on Windows as though the Window andBuffer were one entity and the same size.  The System V 'pad' feature isalso supported.	USING THE MICROWARE SCREEN PACKAGE		The screen package (hereafter referred to as "curses") provides ageneral purpose interface for terminal text display manipulation.  It may beused for simple direct screen control with minimal io overhead (when anupdate is performed, only changed characters are sent to the terminal),or it may be used for multi-window applications.  The number of windows andtheir size is limited by available memory.	The simplest way to create a window is to use the function newwin().This will return a simple window that has a size and screen location asspecified by the programmer.	All input and output is performed through the predefined screen windowstdscr, and/or programmer created windows.  Using the window output functionslike waddch(), waddstr(), etc., the programmer may write text to anywindow and at any location on the terminal screen.  The important thingto note is that no text is actually written to the terminal until anupdate is performed by a call to wrefresh(), allpaint(), or update().	Once text has been written in a window and the programmer desiresthat the text appear on the terminal screen, the window must be updated.There are three ways to perform an update.  The most direct is to callupdate().  This will compare the contents of the window with the screenimage stored in curscr and necessary changes will be sent to the terminal.However, use of update() precludes the use of the advanced functionallpaint().  The second way to update a window is the standard cursesfunction wrefresh().  This function first calls paint() to map theselected window to a virtual screen virscr, and then calls update().Wrefresh() is compatible with allpaint().  Finally, allpaint() is thethird way to update a window.  Allpaint() works by using paint() to addall unhidden windows to virscr, then calls update(virscr).  This isvery efficient way to update multiple windows.  Paint() is synonymouswith the System V function wnoutrefresh(), and a macro exists for this.A macro for System V function doupdate() is also defined.	Input to an application is performed through wgetch() and itsdescendants.  Various functions exist to control the io methods used.For example, the standard io mode uses buffered OS-9 readln calls, butunbuffered io may be used by setting modes with crmode() or raw().echo() and noecho() allow the programmer to control whether typedcharacters are echoed to the terminal.	FUNCTIONS	Standard Curses Functions		Output Functions		addch(ch)	waddch(win, ch)		WINDOW *win;		char ch;		Add the character ch to the window at the current window cursor	coordinates.  Tabs will be expanded to spaces according to the tab	setting in the output path descriptor.		addstr(str)	waddstr(win, str)		WINDOW *win;		char *str;		Add the string pointed to by str to the window at the current window	cursor coordinates.		box(win, vert, hor)		WINDOW *win;		char vert, hor;		Draws a box at the boundary of the window using vert as the	vertical edges and hor as the horizontal edges.		clear()	wclear(win)		WINDOW *win;		Clear the window to blanks and home the cursor to (0,0).  If win	is a screen, the clear flag is set, which will force a clear screen	sequence the next time the window is a parameter to a wrefresh() or	update() (or allpaint() if the window is not hidden).		clearok(win, bf)		WINDOW *win;		bool bf;		Set the clear flag for window win to the value bf.  If true, this	will force a clear screen sequence the next time the window is a	parameter to wrefresh() or update() (or allpaint() if the window is	not hidden).  This has no noticeable effect if the window is not a	screen.  If win is curscr, then a clear screen sequence will occur	the next time any form of screen updating occurs.		clrtobot()	wclrtobot(win)	mvclrtobot(y, x)	mvwclrtobot(win, y, x)		WINDOW *win;		int y, x;		Clear the window to spaces from the current or new (y,x) window	cursor coordinates.  This will not force a clear screen sequence to	the terminal.  The cursor coordinates remain unchanged unless an	mv- call is used.		clrtoeol()	wclrtoeol(win)	mvclrtoeol(y, x)	mvwclrtoeol(win, y, x)		WINDOW *win;		int y, x;		Clear the line to spaces from the current or new (y,x) window	cursor coordinates.  The cursor coordinates remain unchanged unless	an mv- call is used.		delch()	wdelch(win)	mvdelch(y, x)	mvwdelch(win, y, x)			WINDOW *win;		int y, x;		Delete the character in the window at the current or new (y,x)	window cursor coordinates.  Each character on the line to the right	of the cursor is shifted left and the last character becomes blank.	The cursor coordinates remain unchanged unless an mv- call is used.		deleteln()	wdeleteln(win)	mvdeleteln(y, x)	mvwdeleteln(win, y, x)		WINDOW *win;		int y, x;		Delete the line in the window at the current or new (y,x) window	cursor coordinates.  Each line below the line is shifted up and the	bottom line becomes blank. The cursor coordinates remain unchanged	unless an mv- call is used.		erase()	werase(win)		WINDOW *win;		Clear the window to blanks.  Similar to wclear(), except it does	not affect the clear flag or the cursor position.		insch(ch)	winsch(win, ch)	mvinsch(ch)	mvwinsch(win, y, x, ch)		WINDOW *win;		int y, x;		char ch;		Insert ch into the window at the current or new (y,x) cursor	coordinates.  Each character to the right shifts to the right, and	the rightmost character is lost.  The cursor coordinates remain	unchanged unless an mv- call is used.	insertln()	winsertln(win)	mvinsertln(y, x)	mvwinsertln(win, y, x)		WINDOW *win;		int y, x;		Insert a blank line at the current or new (y, x) cursor coordinates.	The current line and all below it are shifted down one line, and the	bottom line is lost.  The cursor coordinates remain unchanged unless	an mv- call is used.		move(y, x)	wmove(win, y, x)		WINDOW *win;		int y, x;		Move the window's cursor to a new position.  Returns ERR if illegal	or non-existant position.		overlay(win1, win2)	overwrite(win1, win2)		WINDOW *win1, *win2;		Write the data in win1 to win2.  Overlay is non-destructive, i.e.,	blanks in win1 are unaffected in win2.  Overwrite is destructive, and	all data in win1 will be copied to win2.  The copy begins at the home	position in each window and will stop when the boundaries of either	window are reached, so it is possible to overlay and overwrite	windows of varying sizes.		printw(fmt, arg1, arg2, ...)	wprintw(win, fmt, arg1, arg2, ...)	mvprintw(y, x, fmt, arg1, arg2, ...)	mvwprintw(win, y, x, fmt, arg1, arg2, ...)		WINDOW *win;		int y, x;		char *fmt;		These functions generate an appropriate printf string and call	waddstr() to write the string to the window.		refresh()	wrefresh(win)		WINDOW *win;		Write the window to the screen.  Wrefresh() actually paints	the window to virscr and then calls update() to put the window	data on the screen.  By painting to virscr, wrefresh works in a	manner compatible with allpaint(), but allows updating of a single	window rather than all windows.  Note that wrefresh() ignores the	hide status of a window.	standout()	wstandout(win)		WINDOW *win;		Start writing characters to win in standout mode.  All subsequent	characters written to the window will be in standout mode.  See	attroff(), etc.		standend()	wstandend(win)		WINDOW *win;		Turn off standout mode.  For System V compatibility, this will	reset all display attributes to off.  See attroff(), etc.			Input functions		crmode()		Set the terminal input routines to use OS-9 read.  Attempts	to simulate the OS-9 equivalent of UNIX's tty driver CBREAK mode.	nocrmode()		Deactivate crmode.  If raw() has not been activated, then the	terminal input routines will use OS-9 readln calls and all line	editing functions will be available.  Default setting.		echo()		Set the terminal path descriptor to echo typed characters.	Default setting.		noecho()		Set the terminal path descriptor to not echo typed characters.	The user program must echo them if desired.		getch()	wgetch(win)	mvgetch(y, x)	mvwgetch(win, y, x)		WINDOW *win;		int y, x;		Get a character from the window at the current cursor position.	The character will be echoed if echo mode is on.  This will return	ERR if it would cause the terminal screen to scroll and curscr's	scroll flag is not set.  If noecho is selected, the window is not	changed.  If crmode or rawmode is selected, wgetch() will return a	character as soon as it is typed, however, if niether crmode or	rawmode has been selected, then wgetch() will not return until an	end-of-line has been typed.  Subsequent wgetch() calls will return	the remaining characters, up to and including the end-of-line	character.		getstr(str)	wgetstr(win, str)	mvgetstr(y, x, str)	mvwgetstr(win, y, x, str)		WINDOW *win;		int y, x;		char *str;		Get a string from the window at the current or new (y,x) cursor	position.  The characters read are copied into str up to an end-of-line	or EOF.  The EOL or EOF are replaced with NULL.		raw()		Turn on raw mode, which deactivates all input buffering and line	editing functionality.		noraw()		Return to normal input and line edit mode.			scanw(fmt, arg1, arg2, ...)	wscanw(win, fmt, arg1, arg2, ...)	mvscanw(y, x, fmt, arg1, arg2, ...)	mvwscanw(win, y, x, fmt, arg1, arg2, ...)		WINDOW *win;		char *fmt;		Perform a scanf() through a window using fmt.		Miscellaneous functions		delwin(win)		WINDOW *win;		Delete the window structure.  If this is the only window accessing	an associated buffer, then the buffer is deleted also.  A subwindow	is not invalidated by the deletion of the parent window, as its buffer	is not changed or deleted.		endwin()		Clean up the windowing system before exit.  All windows and buffers	are deleted, and terminal modes are restored.  It should be called	prior to exiting.		getyx(win, y, x)		WINDOW *win;		int y, x;		Get the window's current cursor coordinates.  Since this is a macro,	use actual variables, not pointers, and beware of side effects.		inch()	winch(win)	mvinch(y, x)	mvwinch(win, y, x)		WINDOW *win;		int y, x;		Returns the character at the current or new (y,x) window	cursor coordinates.		initscr()		Initializes the screen routines.  Initscr() sets the terminal	parameters, gets the termcap information, and allocates space for	the standard window structures, stdscr, virscr, and curscr.  Initscr()	must be called before any of the window routines are called.		leaveok(win, bf)		WINDOW *win;		bool bf;		Sets the flag for leaving the cursor after the last change.  If	the flag is TRUE, then the cursor will be left after the next update	to the terminal, and the current cursor coordinates for the window	will be changed.  If it is FALSE, the hardware cursor will be moved	to reflect the window's cursor coordinates.  Default FALSE.		longname(termbuf, name)		char *termbuf, *name;		Fills name with the full name of the terminal described in	the termcap entry in termbuf.  termbuf is generally filled by the	termlib function tgetent().  The variable ttytype contains the full	name of the terminal currently in use.	mvwin(win, y, x)		WINDOW *win;		int y, x;		Move the home position (in screen coordinates) of a window to the	new coordinates (y,x).  If this would put any portion of the window	over a screen boundary, mvwin() will return ERR and not make any	changes.		WINDOW *newwin(lines, cols, home_line, home_col)		int lines, cols, home_line, home_col;		Create a new window with lines lines and cols columns, and home	screen postion (home_line,home_col).  If either lines or cols is 0,	that dimension will be set to (LINES - home_line) or (COLS - home_col),	respectively.  To get a new window of full screen size (and thus with	its screen attribute set), use newwin(0,0,0,0).		nl()		Set the terminal to newline mapping mode.  This is the normal OS-9	mode wherein a line feed is automatically added to a carriage return.	Default setting.		nonl()		Turn off terminal newline mapping mode.  This attempts to act like	Unix nonl(), and line feed becomes the end of line character and auto	line feed is turned off.		scrollok(win, bf)		WINDOW *win;		bool bf;		Set the scroll flag for a window.  If bf is TRUE, then the window	will be allowed to scroll, if FALSE, the window will not scroll.  FALSE	is the default setting.		touchwin(win)		WINDOW *win;		Set a flag in a window so that the next time it is involved in an	update(), every character in the window gets re-written.  This is	potentially useful for refreshing overlapping windows.		WINDOW *subwin(win, lines, cols, home_line, home_col)		WINDOW *win;		int lines, cols, home_line, home_col;		Create a new window, similar to newwin(), except that the new window	will point into the buffer associated with win.  home_line and home_col	are screen coordinates, so the offset into the buffer will be determined	by the relationship between the screen positions for the two windows.		char *unctrl(ch)		char ch;		This returns a pointer to a string which will either be ch, or, if	ch is a non-printing character, an uppercase character preceeded by a	caret '^'.		gettmode()		Gets the path descriptor status for later use.  Called by initscr().			mvcur(lasty, lastx, newy, newx)		int lasty, lastx, newy, newx;		Move the terminal cursor to (newy,newx).  In order to override the	cursor optimization routines, the (lasty,lastx) pair may be used to	convince the cursor movement routine that the cursor is actually far	away, and force a true absolute cursor positioning sequence.		scroll(win)		WINDOW *win;		Scroll a window up one line.			savetty()		Save the current path descriptor to a safe place.  Called by	initscr().		resetty()		Reset the path descriptor to the settings saved by savetty().	Called by endwin().		setterm(name)		char *name;		Set the terminal characteristics (from termcap) to the terminal	name.  Called by initscr().		tstp()		Not available in Microware curses.	System V compatible functions		wnoutrefresh(win)	paint(win)		WINDOW *win;		Paint the window win onto the virtual display screen, virscr.			doupdate()		A macro for update(virscr).	WINDOW *newpad(rows, cols)		int rows, cols;		Similar to newwin(), but returns a system V compatible pad	structure.  Creates a window and buffer of arbitrary size.		prefresh(pad, pminrow, pmincol, sminrow, smincol, smaxrow, smaxcol)		WINDOW *pad;		int pminrow, pmaxrow, sminrow, smincol, smaxrow, smaxcol;		Equivalent of wrefresh but works with pads.  The arguments specify	the location of the displayed portion of the pad on the screen and	the offsets into the buffer.  pminrow and pmincol specify the	beginning row and column of the buffer to display.  sminrow, smincol,	smaxrow, and smaxcol specify the screen location of the display	window.  The number of rows and columns to display is implied from	the size of the screen window.			pnoutrefresh(pad, pminrow, pmaxrow, sminrow, smincol, smaxrow, smaxcol)		WINDOW *pad;		int pminrow, pmaxrow, sminrow, smincol, smaxrow, smaxcol;		Equivalent of paint, but for a pad.	 See prefresh.		flushinp()		Flushes the input buffer.		nodelay(win, bf)		WINDOW *win;		bool bf;		If bf is TRUE, this causes wgetch to check for data before doing	read.  If no data is present, wgetch will return ERR.  If bf is FALSE	(default), normal io blocking will occur.	cbreak()	nocbreak()		Macros for System V equivalents of crmode() and nocrmode().	typeahead(fd)		FILE *fd;		Wrefresh and update will check for typeahead on input if requested.	Passing any value other than -1 will activate this feature.  Update and	wrefresh will return OK if data is ready before completing a screen	update.   If -1 is passed (default), then screen updates will complete	before further processing may continue.		attroff(attrs)	wattroff(win, attrs)	attron(attrs)	wattron(win, attrs)		attrset(attrs)	wattrset(win, attrs)		WINDOW *win;		int attrs;		These routines alter the display attributes of characters written	to windows.  Attributes are a property of a character and follow the	character through any changes.  All system V attributes (A_STANDOUT,	A_REVERSE, A_BOLD, A_DIM, A_BLINK, and A_UNDERLINE) are defined, but	only A_STANDOUT is supported.  wattroff() turns off the selected	attributes, wattron() turns on the selected attributes, and wattrset()	resets all the attributes to the selected values.	Advanced Functions		shift(win, row, col)		WINDOW *win;		int row, col;		Shift the window win to manipulate and display a new region of its	buffer, starting at (row, col).  Shift() will not allow a window to	go past the edge of a buffer.		resize(win, rows, cols)		WINDOW *win;		int rows, cols;		Change the size of the window to be (rows) deep and (cols) wide.	Resize() will not allow a window to go past the edge of a buffer.  The	screen attribute of a window may be affected by resize().		update(win)		WINDOW *win;		Write the window out to the terminal;		allpaint()		Paint all unhidden windows to the virtual display screen, then	update the virtual screen to the terminal.  Eliminates redundant io	calls when painting multiple windows.  Windows will be painted according	to their location in the window list.  The first window in the list	will have lowest priority and the last window in the list will have	highest priority.		priority(win)		WINDOW *win;		Give window win the highest priority when using allpaint().  This	is accomplished by putting win at the end of the window list.	nopriority(win)		WINDOW *win;		Give window win the lowest priority when using allpaint().  This	is accomplished by putting win at the start of the window list.	hide(win)		WINDOW *win;		Hide a window from allpaint().  Hidden windows will not be painted	when allpaint() is called.		unhide(win)		WINDOW *win;		Unhide a window from allpaint().  Reverses the effect of hide().			cbox(win, vert, hor, corn)		WINDOW *win;		char vert, hor, corn;		Similar to box(), but also allows the specification of the character	to be displayed in the corners.		WINBUF *newbuf(rows, cols)		int rows, cols;		Create a buffer for later association with a window.  The buffer	will be of size (rows, cols).  Returns NULL on error.			WINDOW *w_open(rows, cols, hrow, hcol)		int rows, cols, hrow, hcol;		Create a window header structure.  This is useless until linked	with a buffer with w_linkbuf.  The arguments are simply placed in the	appropriate fields in the WINDOW structure. Note that windows are in	the window master list pointed to by the global variable winhead.	Returns NULL on error.		WINBUF *w_bufopen(cols)		int cols;		Create a buffer header structure.  This buffer will not have any	space for use with a window, and lines must added with w_addline().	Note that buffers are in the buffer master list pointed to by the	global variable bufhead.  Returns NULL on error.		WINDOW *w_linkbuf(win, buf)		WINDOW *win;		WINBUF *buf;		Links a buffer to a window.  Returns win.		char *w_addline(buf)		WINBUF *buf;		Adds a line to the buffer buf.  The line will have room for	buf->b_lsize characters.  Returns a pointer to the text portion	of the line added, or NULL on error.		tbreak()		Checks the typeahead status and returns TRUE if typeahead is	activated and data is ready, FALSE otherwise.	chkin()		Checks the input buffer and input stream.  Returns TRUE if data	is available for reading with getch, FALSE otherwise.`0	